The key error is at the bottom of the traceback:

ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'optical.management'

This means Python is trying to import:

from optical.management.models import Order, OrderItem

but it cannot find a package called optical.management.

Trace the error backwards

The import chain is:

manage.py runserver
    ↓
optical/urls.py
    ↓
include('management.urls')
    ↓
management/urls.py
    ↓
from . import views
    ↓
management/views.py
    ↓
from .serializers import ...
    ↓
management/serializers.py
    ↓
from services.order_service import OrderService
    ↓
services/order_service.py
    ↓
from optical.management.models import Order, OrderItem   ← CRASH

The actual failure occurs in:

services/order_service.py
line 134
Why this happens

Your project structure is probably something like:

final_build_system_optic/
│
├── optical/
│   ├── manage.py
│   ├── management/
│   │   ├── models.py
│   │   ├── views.py
│   │   └── serializers.py
│   │
│   └── optical/
│       ├── settings.py
│       └── urls.py
│
└── services/
    └── order_service.py

Notice:

management/

is an app beside the inner optical project package.

But your code imports:

from optical.management.models import Order, OrderItem

which assumes this structure:

optical/
    management/
        models.py

inside the Python package optical.

Python cannot find:

optical.management

because the app is probably registered simply as:

management

not

optical.management
Most likely fix

Open:

services/order_service.py

Find:

from optical.management.models import Order, OrderItem

Change to:

from management.models import Order, OrderItem

if your app is named management.

Verify the app name

Look in:

management/apps.py

Example:

class ManagementConfig(AppConfig):
    default_auto_field = 'django.db.models.BigAutoField'
    name = 'management'

If it says:

name = 'management'

then imports should be:

from management.models import ...

not

from optical.management.models import ...
Another possibility

If your directory structure is:

optical/
    management/
        models.py

then the app may actually be inside the package.

In that case verify that:

optical/
    __init__.py
    management/
        __init__.py
        models.py

exist.

Missing __init__.py files can also make imports fail.

Quick diagnostic

Run from the project root:

python manage.py shell

and test:

import management

If this works:

from management.models import Order

then the correct import is:

from management.models import Order, OrderItem

If instead:

import optical.management

works, then the package structure is different.

